/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046647 #edb/ref 1007 "Mathematical Foundations II" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046647 #edb/ref 1008 "Mathematical Foundations II is the second course in a sequence specially crafted for adults seeking the most direct path to prepare for university math courses. Building on the basic algebra covered in Mathematical Foundations I , students in Mathematical Foundations II generalize their understanding of algebra to more advanced functions (including trigonometry) and become acquainted with limits and derivatives in calculus. Upon completing the course, students will be prepared for Mathematical Foundations III , the final stepping stone in preparing for university-level math." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046647 #edb/ref 1009 "Master the algebra of advanced functions including quadratics, logarithms, and trigonometry. Dive deep into the theory of polynomials, learn the basics of limits, derivatives, and integrals from calculus, and explore a variety of concepts from higher math including complex numbers, vectors, probability, and statistics." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046647 #edb/ref 1012 111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046647 #edb/ref 1013 "MF2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046647 #edb/ref 1014 #uuid "b5d8b420-892a-5b6a-8150-ba3c70c4d779" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048338 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048338 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048338 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048338 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048338 #edb/ref 1031 "Finding the Common Difference of an Arithmetic Sequence" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048338 #edb/ref 1033 669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048338 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "96be3b68-ea0a-594d-ac9b-2bd958c09c72" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048765 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048765 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078448 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048765 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078449 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048765 #edb/ref 1031 "Solving for Variables in Arithmetic Sequences" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048765 #edb/ref 1033 670 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048765 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "bcfdff82-87ca-554b-a5c6-f8dafa1608ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048938 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048938 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048938 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048938 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048938 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186133962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048938 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186133963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048938 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186133964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048938 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186133965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048938 #edb/ref 1031 "Evaluating Rational Expressions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048938 #edb/ref 1033 2183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048938 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "cd61cb00-4988-54d1-a596-e98a1a9c15ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049504 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049504 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049504 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049504 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049504 #edb/ref 1031 "Finding the Nth Term of an Arithmetic Sequence Given Two Terms" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049504 #edb/ref 1033 1346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049504 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "fa26fae5-4dd8-54f6-8b10-f83839bbfcdb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049579 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049579 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049579 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049579 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049579 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049579 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049579 #edb/ref 1031 "Arithmetic Sequences" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049579 #edb/ref 1033 667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049579 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "01b16520-9a60-5cca-93f8-78c461d28525" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078183 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14385" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078183 #edb/ref 1071 "An arithmetic sequence has $a_{11}=39$ and $a_{12}=34$. What is the common difference of the sequence?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078183 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cb23119e-7625-5dad-873f-03049677618c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078183 #edb/ref 1074 "The first thing to note is that the terms are *decreasing*, and therefore we expect the common difference $d$ to be *negative*.\n\nSince the two terms are consecutive ($a_{12}$ goes right after $a_{11}$ in the sequence), we can find the common difference by subtracting $a_{11}$ from $a_{12}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{12}-{a}_{11} \\\\  & =34-39 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIndeed, our result is negative, so we can be fairly confident that it is correct." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078185 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14390" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078185 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the common difference of an arithmetic sequence if the $12$th term is $-56$ and the $20$th term is $-112.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078185 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a22637f2-14e5-55ff-a41b-1f2a7e0eaa73" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078185 #edb/ref 1074 "The first thing to note is that the terms are *decreasing*, and therefore we expect $d$ to be *negative*.\n\nWe are given the terms\n\n$$\na_{\\color{black}12} = {\\color{red}-56}, \\quad a_{\\color{black}20} = {\\color{blue}-112}.\n$$\n\nTo find $d,$ we apply the formula with $n=20$ and $m=12\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{n}-{a}_{m}}{n-m} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{20}-{a}_{12}}{20-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{-112-(-56)}{20-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{-112+56}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{-56}{8} \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common difference is $d=-7.$ \n\nIndeed, our result is negative, so we can be fairly confident that it is correct." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078423 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078423 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186078779 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078423 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186078787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078423 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186079080 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078423 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186519529 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078423 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186519564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078423 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186519575 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078423 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Common Difference Given Two Consecutive Terms" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078423 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186078183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078423 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f9307a5e-2eea-5cea-b5f3-2ce6df53961e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078424 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048938 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078424 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186078772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078424 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186078788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078424 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186078789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078424 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186519596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078424 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186519606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078424 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Common Difference Given Two Non-Consecutive Terms" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078424 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186078185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078424 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4d15f023-4061-51ad-b307-013bd06d3ec1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078685 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078685 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078685 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078685 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078685 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078685 #edb/ref 1045 "Finding the Common Difference of an Arithmetic Sequence" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078685 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186079292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078685 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078685 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078685 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "d2b403a1-8031-5a79-8235-5b648d030a5b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079292 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079292 #edb/ref 1061 148381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079292 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d8a0caf7-c1c8-5350-9d6b-8db3c51bdd00" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079292 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186079728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079293 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079293 #edb/ref 1061 148382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079293 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ca7d2f7b-9202-580e-bad9-b64525e020c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079293 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079294 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079294 #edb/ref 1061 148386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079294 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "59dd0405-0ca2-5664-bbd2-78d0a305318e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079294 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186079729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079295 #edb/ref 1061 148387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079295 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2f2df9f7-ad2a-5436-aa72-25df975ccb0b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079295 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079728 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that the fourth term of an arithmetic sequence is $6$ and the fifth term is $12.$ Can we use this information to compute the common difference of the sequence?\n\nThe first thing to note is that the sequence is *increasing*, and therefore we expect the common difference $d$ to be positive.\n\nNow, let's take a look at the jumps:\n\n![](images/6b/6b82ba58c986aa32635e2ebbd8b1e0fd0affdbc81e35a015960777fa72d5f72b.png)\n\nBecause $a_4 = 6$ and $a_5 = 12$ are **consecutive terms**, meaning that $a_5$ comes straight after $a_4$ in the sequence, we can easily compute the common difference $d$ by subtracting $a_4$ from $a_5\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{5}-{a}_{4} \\\\  & =12-6 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079728 #edb/ref 1065 8911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079728 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e69a6b27-01d4-5ee2-81c3-d7d8f834b087" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079728 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079729 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that we're given the second and fifth terms only of an arithmetic sequence. For example, suppose that we know that\n\n$$\na_2 = {\\color{red}8}, \\quad a_5 = {\\color{red}23}\n$$\n\nThese terms are not consecutive, and so how can we figure out the common difference $d?$\n\nFirst, let's take a look at the jumps:\n\n![](images/ff/ff83c75935acca7ddd5c421cd81fad85685c68fd782d1d832c068237c751753d.png)\n\nWe see that between ${\\color{red}8}$ and ${\\color{red}23}$, there are a total of ${\\color{blue}3}$ jumps. So, to find the value of a single jump, we can take the total difference and divide it by the number of jumps:\n\n$$\nd = \\frac{{\\color{red}23}\\,-\\,{\\color{red}8}}{\\color{blue}3} = \\dfrac{15}{3} = 5\n$$\n\nWe can check that our result $d=5$ is correct by using it to figure out the intermediate terms. Indeed, the common difference $d=5$ gets us from $a_2={\\color{red}8}$ to $a_5={\\color{red}23}$ in ${\\color{blue}3}$ jumps:\n\n![](images/7a/7aa192675aeb3c7be78617035b74362b38974e8501d95c0692238811ae4769f1.png)\n\nIn general, if we're given two terms $a_n$ and $a_m$ of an arithmetic sequence, then we can calculate the common difference using the following formula:\n\n$$\nd = \\frac{a_n-a_m}{n-m}\n$$\n\nUsing this formula to calculate the common difference between $a_{2}=8$ and $a_{5}=23,$ we get the same result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{5}-{a}_{2}}{5-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{23-8}{5-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{3} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079729 #edb/ref 1065 8913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079729 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "dab8ebfb-480a-5a36-9a12-169a69cd8316" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079729 #edb/ref 1067 "Non-Consecutive Terms" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519021 #edb/ref 1080 "5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519021 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519021 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "12893f67-0a72-530f-9e9f-da2d3170d036" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519036 #edb/ref 1080 "-4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519036 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519036 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1c65c842-a24a-534b-b71d-7bbd9a2e778b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519073 #edb/ref 1080 "8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519073 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519073 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "34e48f07-bceb-5ad2-80b0-5117a66ddea5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519107 #edb/ref 1080 "-3.25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519107 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519107 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "488a956f-9bc7-5837-8db3-c481d1b56fc8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519115 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186